Creating Dynamic Dashboards with Variables
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Understand the concept and benefits of dashboard variables in Grafana
Create different types of variables including query variables, custom variables, and constant variables
Configure variable settings such as refresh options, multi-value selection, and include all options
Use variables in panel queries to create dynamic content
Apply variables in panel titles and annotations for enhanced dashboard interactivity
Implement cascading variables for hierarchical data filtering
Test and validate variable functionality across different dashboard panels
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with Grafana dashboard creation and panel configuration
Knowledge of PromQL (Prometheus Query Language) basics
Understanding of time series data concepts
Experience with basic system monitoring concepts
Lab Environment Setup
Note: Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will install all required components during this lab.

Task 1: Environment Preparation and Tool Installation
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Dependencies
First, update your system and install necessary dependencies:

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y wget curl software-properties-common apt-transport-https
Subtask 1.2: Install Docker for Container Management
Install Docker to run Prometheus and Grafana:

curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg
echo "deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
Log out and log back in, or run:

newgrp docker
Subtask 1.3: Create Project Directory Structure
Create a structured directory for the lab:

mkdir -p ~/grafana-variables-lab/{prometheus,grafana,data}
cd ~/grafana-variables-lab
Subtask 1.4: Configure Prometheus
Create a Prometheus configuration file:

cat > prometheus/prometheus.yml << 'EOF'
global:
  scrape_interval: 15s
  evaluation_interval: 15s

rule_files:
  # - "first_rules.yml"
  # - "second_rules.yml"

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']
    scrape_interval: 5s

  - job_name: 'node-exporter'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['node-exporter:9100']
    scrape_interval: 5s

  - job_name: 'sample-app-1'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['sample-app-1:8080']
    scrape_interval: 5s
    labels:
      environment: 'production'
      region: 'us-east-1'
      team: 'backend'

  - job_name: 'sample-app-2'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['sample-app-2:8080']
    scrape_interval: 5s
    labels:
      environment: 'staging'
      region: 'us-west-2'
      team: 'frontend'

  - job_name: 'sample-app-3'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['sample-app-3:8080']
    scrape_interval: 5s
    labels:
      environment: 'development'
      region: 'eu-west-1'
      team: 'backend'
EOF
Subtask 1.5: Create Docker Compose Configuration
Create a comprehensive Docker Compose file:

cat > docker-compose.yml << 'EOF'
version: '3.8'

services:
  prometheus:
    image: prom/prometheus:latest
    container_name: prometheus
    ports:
      - "9090:9090"
    volumes:
      - ./prometheus/prometheus.yml:/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
      - prometheus_data:/prometheus
    command:
      - '--config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml'
      - '--storage.tsdb.path=/prometheus'
      - '--web.console.libraries=/etc/prometheus/console_libraries'
      - '--web.console.templates=/etc/prometheus/consoles'
      - '--storage.tsdb.retention.time=200h'
      - '--web.enable-lifecycle'
    networks:
      - monitoring

  grafana:
    image: grafana/grafana:latest
    container_name: grafana
    ports:
      - "3000:3000"
    volumes:
      - grafana_data:/var/lib/grafana
      - ./grafana/provisioning:/etc/grafana/provisioning
    environment:
      - GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin123
      - GF_USERS_ALLOW_SIGN_UP=false
    networks:
      - monitoring

  node-exporter:
    image: prom/node-exporter:latest
    container_name: node-exporter
    ports:
      - "9100:9100"
    volumes:
      - /proc:/host/proc:ro
      - /sys:/host/sys:ro
      - /:/rootfs:ro
    command:
      - '--path.procfs=/host/proc'
      - '--path.rootfs=/rootfs'
      - '--path.sysfs=/host/sys'
      - '--collector.filesystem.mount-points-exclude=^/(sys|proc|dev|host|etc)($$|/)'
    networks:
      - monitoring

  sample-app-1:
    image: prom/prometheus:latest
    container_name: sample-app-1
    ports:
      - "8081:9090"
    command:
      - '--config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml'
      - '--storage.tsdb.path=/prometheus'
      - '--web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:8080'
    networks:
      - monitoring

  sample-app-2:
    image: prom/prometheus:latest
    container_name: sample-app-2
    ports:
      - "8082:9090"
    command:
      - '--config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml'
      - '--storage.tsdb.path=/prometheus'
      - '--web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:8080'
    networks:
      - monitoring

  sample-app-3:
    image: prom/prometheus:latest
    container_name: sample-app-3
    ports:
      - "8083:9090"
    command:
      - '--config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml'
      - '--storage.tsdb.path=/prometheus'
      - '--web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:8080'
    networks:
      - monitoring

volumes:
  prometheus_data:
  grafana_data:

networks:
  monitoring:
    driver: bridge
EOF
Subtask 1.6: Start the Monitoring Stack
Launch all services:

docker-compose up -d
Verify all containers are running:

docker-compose ps
Wait for all services to be healthy (approximately 2-3 minutes):

sleep 180
Task 2: Create Variables in Grafana
Subtask 2.1: Access Grafana and Initial Setup
Open your web browser and navigate to Grafana:

echo "Access Grafana at: http://localhost:3000"
echo "Username: admin"
echo "Password: admin123"
Log in to Grafana using the credentials above
Navigate to Configuration > Data Sources
Click Add data source
Select Prometheus
Set the URL to: http://prometheus:9090
Click Save & Test
Subtask 2.2: Create a New Dashboard
Click the + icon in the left sidebar
Select Dashboard
Click Add new panel
In the panel editor, click Save (disk icon)
Name the dashboard: Dynamic Variables Dashboard
Click Save
Subtask 2.3: Create Your First Variable - Job Variable
Click the Dashboard settings gear icon in the top right
Select Variables from the left menu
Click Add variable
Configure the Job variable:

Name: job
Type: Query
Label: Job
Data source: Prometheus
Query: label_values(up, job)
Regex: Leave empty
Sort: Alphabetical (asc)
Multi-value: Enable (check the box)
Include All option: Enable (check the box)
Custom all value: Leave empty
Click Update to save the variable.

Subtask 2.4: Create Environment Variable
Click Add variable again and configure:

Name: environment
Type: Query
Label: Environment
Data source: Prometheus
Query: label_values(up, environment)
Regex: Leave empty
Sort: Alphabetical (asc)
Multi-value: Enable
Include All option: Enable
Click Update to save.

Subtask 2.5: Create Region Variable
Add another variable:

Name: region
Type: Query
Label: Region
Data source: Prometheus
Query: label_values(up, region)
Regex: Leave empty
Sort: Alphabetical (asc)
Multi-value: Enable
Include All option: Enable
Click Update to save.

Subtask 2.6: Create Instance Variable with Cascading
Create a cascading variable that depends on the job selection:

Name: instance
Type: Query
Label: Instance
Data source: Prometheus
Query: label_values(up{job=~"$job"}, instance)
Regex: Leave empty
Sort: Alphabetical (asc)
Multi-value: Enable
Include All option: Enable
Click Update to save.

Subtask 2.7: Create Custom Interval Variable
Add a custom variable for time intervals:

Name: interval
Type: Custom
Label: Interval
Values separated by comma: 5m,10m,30m,1h,6h,12h,1d
Multi-value: Disable
Include All option: Disable
Click Update to save.

Click Save dashboard to save all variables.

Task 3: Use Variables to Create Dynamic Dashboard Panels
Subtask 3.1: Create CPU Usage Panel with Variables
Click Add panel in the dashboard
Select Add a new panel
In the query editor, enter:
rate(prometheus_tsdb_head_samples_appended_total{job=~"$job", instance=~"$instance"}[$interval])
Set the panel title to: CPU Usage - Job: $job, Instance: $instance

In the Panel options:

Title: CPU Usage - Job: $job, Instance: $instance
Description: Dynamic CPU usage metrics filtered by selected job and instance
Click Apply to save the panel

Subtask 3.2: Create Memory Usage Panel
Add another panel:

Click Add panel > Add a new panel
Enter the query:
prometheus_tsdb_head_series{job=~"$job", instance=~"$instance"}
Configure the panel:

Title: Memory Series - $job ($region)
Description: Memory metrics for job $job in region $region
Click Apply

Subtask 3.3: Create Network Traffic Panel
Add a third panel:

Click Add panel > Add a new panel
Enter the query:
rate(prometheus_http_requests_total{job=~"$job", instance=~"$instance"}[$interval])
Configure the panel:

Title: HTTP Requests Rate - $environment Environment
Description: HTTP request rate for $environment environment over $interval intervals
In the Legend section, set:

Legend: {{job}} - {{instance}}
Click Apply

Subtask 3.4: Create Status Overview Panel
Create a stat panel:

Click Add panel > Add a new panel
Change visualization type to Stat
Enter the query:
count(up{job=~"$job", environment=~"$environment", region=~"$region"} == 1)
Configure the panel:

Title: Active Services
Description: Number of active services matching current filters
In Value options:

Unit: Short
Decimals: 0
Click Apply

Subtask 3.5: Create Table Panel with Multiple Metrics
Add a table panel:

Click Add panel > Add a new panel
Change visualization to Table
Add multiple queries:
Query A:

up{job=~"$job", environment=~"$environment", region=~"$region"}
Query B (click + Query):

prometheus_build_info{job=~"$job", instance=~"$instance"}
Configure the panel:

Title: Service Status Table - $job
Description: Detailed status information for selected services
Click Apply

Subtask 3.6: Save and Test the Dashboard
Click Save dashboard (disk icon)
Add a description: Dashboard demonstrating dynamic variables with Prometheus metrics
Click Save
